Single arm, 100 lines, 4 table-driven tests, clippy 0.

Count says nothing about a table-driven suite, so I probed all seven behaviours
against the built module. Every one correct, including the two that carry it:

  0 with empty contract   -> Done           proceeds=true  caches=true
  0 LISTED as N/A         -> Done           proceeds=true  caches=true
  4 listed                -> NotApplicable  proceeds=true  caches=FALSE
  4 unlisted              -> Failed(4)      proceeds=false caches=false
  duplicate 4,4           -> NotApplicable
  -9 signal kill          -> Failed(-9)     preserved verbatim
  i32::MIN                -> Failed         preserved verbatim

Clause 5 is the whole point: NotApplicable PROCEEDS but does NOT CACHE. Nothing
was produced, so recording a freshness signature would make the next run skip a
stage that never ran -- and an implementation returning the same answer for both
questions passes clause 4 alone.

Zero hardcoded stage names in the file: the four real contracts belong to the
caller, so a fifth stage does not require editing this module.

